<header class="lesson">
  <div class="crumb">Lesson 0005 &middot; cpp_games / docs / learning</div>
  <h1>Multithreading and shared resources</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: your <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> already runs two threads (the game loop and a worker) and they pass data through shared memory. Every primitive in this lesson is sitting in <code class="inline">LlmClient.cpp</code> right now &mdash; mutex, condition variable, atomic, joinable thread. This lesson is the <em>why</em> behind what you already have.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept &mdash; what threads share, and what goes wrong</h2>

<p>A <b>thread</b> is an independent stream of execution. When you start one inside your program, it runs alongside the main thread on a different CPU core (most of the time). They can both be doing useful work simultaneously. <em>And</em> &mdash; this is the part that gets you in trouble &mdash; they share the same memory.</p>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>What's shared between threads in one process?</th><th>What's private to each thread?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The heap. <code class="inline">std::vector</code>, <code class="inline">std::queue</code>, any object reachable through a pointer or reference.</td><td>The stack. Local variables and the call chain that produced them.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Global and static variables.</td><td>CPU registers, including the program counter (where in the code this thread is).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Open file descriptors, sockets, anything kernel-tracked.</td><td>Whatever's currently in this thread's registers.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Shared mutable state is the source of every multithreading bug. The classic disaster:</p>

<div class="timeline">      time -->

  Thread A:    read counter (= 5)        ... add 1 ... write counter (= 6)
  Thread B:                read counter (= 5) ... add 1 ... write counter (= 6)

  Two increments happened. The counter is 6 instead of 7.
  That's a <b>race condition</b>.</div>

<p>The fix is <b>synchronisation</b> &mdash; tools that force threads to take turns where it matters. Modern C++ gives you three you'll meet today:</p>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>Primitive</th><th>What it does</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b><code class="inline">std::mutex</code></b></td><td>Only one thread at a time can hold the lock. Others wait at the door.</td><td>Protecting a non-trivial data structure (a queue, a vector, a struct with multiple fields).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><code class="inline">std::condition_variable</code></b></td><td>Lets a thread sleep cheaply until another thread says "wake up, the thing you were waiting for is ready."</td><td>"Wait until the queue has work" / "wait until shutdown is signalled."</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b><code class="inline">std::atomic&lt;T&gt;</code></b></td><td>A single variable whose read/modify/write is uninterruptible by another thread. Hardware-supported.</td><td>One simple value: a counter, a flag, a pointer swap.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout warn">
  <h3>The rule of thumb</h3>
  <p>Use <b>atomic</b> for a single variable. Use <b>mutex</b> for anything bigger. Use a <b>condition variable</b> on top of a mutex when one thread needs to <em>wait</em> for a state change rather than poll.</p>
</div>

<h2>2. Minimal example &mdash; the same counter, three ways</h2>

<p>Two threads each try to increment a counter a million times. We expect 2,000,000.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="com">// (1) NO SYNC — race condition. Final value is unpredictable, usually low.</span>
<span class="bad"><span class="typ">int</span> counter = <span class="num">0</span>;
<span class="kw">auto</span> worker = [&amp;] { <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="typ">int</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; <span class="num">1'000'000</span>; ++i) ++counter; };
std::thread t1(worker), t2(worker);
t1.join(); t2.join();
std::cout &lt;&lt; counter;   <span class="com">// e.g. 1,437,221. Different every run. Wrong.</span></span>

<span class="com">// (2) MUTEX — correct, ~50× slower for this trivial workload.</span>
<span class="ok"><span class="typ">int</span> counter = <span class="num">0</span>;
std::mutex m;
<span class="kw">auto</span> worker = [&amp;] {
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="typ">int</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; <span class="num">1'000'000</span>; ++i) {
        std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(m);  <span class="com">// take lock</span>
        ++counter;                            <span class="com">// safe</span>
    }                                         <span class="com">// lock released here</span>
};</span>

<span class="com">// (3) ATOMIC — correct, near native speed. The right tool here.</span>
<span class="ok">std::atomic&lt;<span class="typ">int</span>&gt; counter{<span class="num">0</span>};
<span class="kw">auto</span> worker = [&amp;] {
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="typ">int</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; <span class="num">1'000'000</span>; ++i) {
        counter.fetch_add(<span class="num">1</span>);                <span class="com">// uninterruptible RMW</span>
    }
};</span></pre>

<p>Three things to take away from this example:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>"It works on my machine"</b> is meaningless for thread bugs. Version 1 will sometimes print 2,000,000 by sheer luck. Race conditions are not deterministic.</li>
  <li><b>The mutex is correct but wasteful here.</b> We're locking and unlocking a million times to protect a single <code class="inline">int</code>. That's what atomics exist for.</li>
  <li><b>The atomic version isn't free.</b> <code class="inline">fetch_add</code> compiles to a special CPU instruction (e.g. <code class="inline">LOCK XADD</code> on x86). It's much faster than a mutex but still ~10&times; slower than a plain <code class="inline">++</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. In your project &mdash; the four primitives in <code class="inline">LlmClient</code></h2>

<p>Open <code class="inline">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.hpp</code>. The bottom of the class is a small museum of every concept above.</p>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.hpp:74</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">private</span>:
    <span class="com">// ATOMIC FLAGS — single bools read/written from both threads.</span>
    std::atomic&lt;<span class="typ">bool</span>&gt; stop_{<span class="kw">false</span>};
    std::atomic&lt;<span class="typ">bool</span>&gt; inFlight_{<span class="kw">false</span>};

    <span class="com">// MUTEX + CV + QUEUE — main thread submits, worker dequeues.</span>
    std::mutex requestMutex_;
    std::condition_variable requestCv_;
    std::queue&lt;ChatRequest&gt; requests_;

    <span class="com">// SECOND MUTEX — protects the reply list independently.</span>
    std::mutex replyMutex_;
    std::vector&lt;ChatReply&gt; replies_;

    <span class="com">// ATOMIC COUNTER — generating unique request IDs without a mutex.</span>
    std::atomic&lt;std::uint64_t&gt; nextId_{<span class="num">1</span>};

    std::thread worker_;</pre>

<h3>The thread itself: RAII lifetime</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:13</small>
<pre class="code">LlmClient::LlmClient(LlmConfig config) : config_(std::move(config)) {
    worker_ = std::thread(&amp;LlmClient::workerLoop, <span class="kw">this</span>);   <span class="com">// start</span>
}

LlmClient::~LlmClient() {
    stop_.store(<span class="kw">true</span>);          <span class="com">// signal: time to leave</span>
    requestCv_.notify_all();    <span class="com">// wake worker if it's sleeping</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();   <span class="com">// wait for it to finish</span>
}</pre>

<p>The thread is owned by the object. Constructor starts it, destructor joins it. If we forgot the <code class="inline">join()</code>, the program would <code class="inline">std::terminate()</code> when the thread object went out of scope while still running. RAII for thread lifetime is just as important as RAII for memory.</p>

<h3>Producer side: submit a request</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:21</small>
<pre class="code">std::uint64_t LlmClient::submit(std::string systemPrompt,
                                std::vector&lt;ChatTurn&gt; history,
                                std::string userMessage) {
    ChatRequest req;
    req.id = nextId_.fetch_add(<span class="num">1</span>);   <span class="com">// ATOMIC: no mutex needed</span>
    <span class="com">// ... move parameters into req ...</span>

    {
        std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(requestMutex_);  <span class="com">// MUTEX: take lock</span>
        requests_.push(std::move(req));                   <span class="com">// modify shared queue</span>
    }                                                     <span class="com">// release at scope end</span>
    requestCv_.notify_one();   <span class="com">// CV: wake up one waiter (the worker)</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> req.id;
}</pre>

<p>Two synchronisation primitives in one function. <code class="inline">nextId_</code> is atomic because incrementing a counter is the textbook atomic use case &mdash; cheaper than a mutex. The queue is mutex-protected because it's a non-trivial data structure with multiple pointers being shuffled around inside <code class="inline">push()</code>. The notify wakes the worker if it was asleep waiting for work.</p>

<h3>Consumer side: the worker waits properly</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:48</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">void</span> LlmClient::workerLoop() {
    <span class="kw">while</span> (<span class="kw">true</span>) {
        ChatRequest req;
        {
            std::unique_lock&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(requestMutex_);

            <span class="com">// CV WITH PREDICATE — sleeps if predicate is false,</span>
            <span class="com">// re-checks every wakeup, ignores spurious wakeups for free.</span>
            requestCv_.wait(lock, [<span class="kw">this</span>] {
                <span class="kw">return</span> stop_.load() || !requests_.empty();
            });

            <span class="kw">if</span> (stop_.load() &amp;&amp; requests_.empty()) <span class="kw">return</span>;
            req = std::move(requests_.front());
            requests_.pop();
        }   <span class="com">// release lock before doing slow work</span>

        ChatReply reply = processOne(req);  <span class="com">// no lock held — others can submit</span>

        {
            std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(replyMutex_);
            replies_.push_back(std::move(reply));
        }
    }
}</pre>

<p>Four design decisions to notice:</p>

<ol>
  <li><b>The CV uses the predicate form of <code class="inline">wait</code>.</b> The version without a predicate is a trap &mdash; OS-level CVs are allowed to wake threads <em>spuriously</em> (no notify happened, the thread just woke up anyway). With a predicate, the standard library re-checks the condition and goes back to sleep if it wasn't really ready. We don't have to write that loop ourselves.</li>
  <li><b>The predicate checks two things.</b> <code class="inline">stop_</code> OR <code class="inline">!requests_.empty()</code>. So the worker wakes on either "shutdown" or "new work". One CV handles both.</li>
  <li><b>The lock is released before <code class="inline">processOne()</code>.</b> Otherwise the worker would hold <code class="inline">requestMutex_</code> for the entire 1-3 second HTTP call to Ollama, and any thread calling <code class="inline">submit()</code> would block. The scope of the lock is exactly "manipulate the queue" &mdash; not "do the actual work."</li>
  <li><b>The reply uses a different mutex.</b> The worker doesn't need <code class="inline">requestMutex_</code> while writing to <code class="inline">replies_</code>, and the main thread (calling <code class="inline">drainReplies()</code>) doesn't need <code class="inline">replyMutex_</code> while calling <code class="inline">submit()</code>. Two independent locks = no false sharing of contention.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Why two mutexes and not one</h3>

<p>Imagine if both vectors lived under a single <code class="inline">stateMutex_</code>. Every <code class="inline">submit()</code> would briefly contend with every <code class="inline">drainReplies()</code> even though they touch <em>different data</em>. With two mutexes:</p>

<div class="timeline">  Single mutex:
    main thread  |--submit--||--drain--|
    worker       :----wait-----: |--push-result--|

  Two mutexes:
    main thread  |--submit--||--drain--|
    worker       :wait: |---http call (no lock)---| |push-result|

  The worker can hold replyMutex_ to write a result *while* the main
  thread is holding requestMutex_ to submit a new request. They don't
  block each other.</div>

<h2>4. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3>Mutex vs. atomic</h3>
<p>People reach for atomics because they sound faster. They are &mdash; <em>for one variable</em>. The moment you need to update two fields together ("subtract from account A and add to account B"), atomic stops being correct on its own; you'd need a mutex around both updates so they appear atomic <em>together</em>. Atomic is for single values that can be read/modified/written in one CPU instruction. Mutex is for anything bigger or any case where invariants span multiple variables.</p>

<h3>Race condition vs. deadlock</h3>
<p>A <b>race condition</b> is two threads stepping on each other's data because neither waited. The program runs but produces wrong results. A <b>deadlock</b> is two threads each holding a lock the other one needs; they wait forever. The program freezes producing nothing. Different failure modes, different fixes: more synchronisation prevents races; <em>less</em> or smarter synchronisation prevents deadlocks. The cardinal rule for avoiding deadlocks is: <em>always acquire locks in the same order</em>. Our <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> never holds both mutexes at once, so deadlock is impossible there.</p>

<h3>notify_one vs. notify_all</h3>
<p>Our <code class="inline">submit()</code> calls <code class="inline">notify_one</code> &mdash; there's only one worker, so waking one is exactly right. <code class="inline">notify_all</code> is for when you've changed the state in a way <em>multiple</em> waiters might care about (e.g. "the connection is now open; everyone who was waiting can proceed"). Using <code class="inline">notify_all</code> when <code class="inline">notify_one</code> would do creates a <b>thundering herd</b>: every waiter wakes, contends for the mutex, finds nothing to do, and goes back to sleep. The destructor uses <code class="inline">notify_all</code> in case we ever add a second worker; with only one waiter today it's equivalent.</p>

<h3>CV vs. polling with sleep</h3>
<p>A tempting alternative for the worker loop is <code class="inline">while(!hasWork) sleep_for(1ms);</code>. It would even work. But the worker is awake every millisecond doing nothing &mdash; burning CPU and battery, and adding up to a millisecond of latency when work arrives mid-sleep. The CV version is <em>asleep</em> &mdash; the OS removes it from the scheduler entirely. It wakes only when notified. Lower latency, lower power, less code.</p>

<h3>Threads vs. processes</h3>
<p>Lesson 0004 mentioned this in passing. Threads share memory; processes don't. Your game and Ollama are two processes &mdash; they can't even see each other's <code class="inline">std::queue</code>, which is why we need HTTP to communicate. Your main thread and worker thread are inside <em>one</em> process &mdash; they share <code class="inline">requests_</code> directly, no serialisation, no syscall. That's why threading is the right tool for the producer/consumer pattern inside <code class="inline">LlmClient</code>, and processes are the right tool for talking to a totally separate program like Ollama.</p>

<h2>5. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You add a debug counter <code class="inline">int requestsSubmitted</code> to <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> and bump it inside <code class="inline">submit()</code>. The class is now multithread-buggy. What's the smallest correct fix?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="It would work, but you'd be locking around a one-instruction increment. Atomic is the cheaper correct fix.">
      Wrap the increment in a <code class="inline">std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. A single integer with read-modify-write is exactly the atomic use case. fetch_add(1) is one CPU instruction with hardware-guaranteed atomicity — cheaper than a mutex and correct.">
      Change the type to <code class="inline">std::atomic&lt;int&gt;</code> and use <code class="inline">fetch_add(1)</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="volatile prevents the compiler from caching the value in a register, but it does NOT make the increment atomic. Two threads can still tear or race. This is the classic 'volatile means thread-safe' myth.">
      Mark it <code class="inline">volatile int</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Putting it in static storage doesn't change anything about access — multiple threads still race on it.">
      Move it to a static variable so only one thread can write it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Why does the worker in <code class="inline">workerLoop()</code> release <code class="inline">requestMutex_</code> before calling <code class="inline">processOne()</code>?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="processOne uses cpp-httplib, which makes its own system calls and doesn't need our mutex. The reason for releasing is performance, not requirement.">
      <code class="inline">processOne</code> needs the mutex internally, so holding it from outside would deadlock.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. processOne takes seconds because it's waiting on Ollama. If we held requestMutex_ that whole time, every submit() from the main thread would block — even though they're touching the same queue we already dequeued from. Locks should cover only the manipulation of the shared data, not the slow work derived from it.">
      So that other threads calling <code class="inline">submit()</code> can keep enqueueing while the slow HTTP call is happening.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The compiler can't release a mutex on its own — that would be a bug in the language. RAII releases at scope end, deterministically.">
      Because the compiler will optimize away the mutex if it sees the call.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Mutexes don't have built-in timers in C++. The release here is structural, governed by scope, not by elapsed time.">
      To allow the mutex's internal timer to expire and avoid a deadlock.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">The worker loop's wait line is <code class="inline">requestCv_.wait(lock, [this] { return stop_.load() || !requests_.empty(); });</code>. Why include the predicate and not just <code class="inline">wait(lock)</code>?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Notifications fire on call. There's no syntactic-sugar reason to write a predicate; it's a semantic one.">
      Predicates are syntactic sugar that lets <code class="inline">notify_one</code> work without a lambda.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Without the predicate, a spurious wakeup (the OS waking us with no notify having happened) would drop us out of wait() and proceed to pop from an empty queue. The predicate form re-acquires the lock, re-checks the condition, and goes back to sleep if it wasn't really ready — for free.">
      Condition variables can wake spuriously (with no notify). The predicate re-checks and goes back to sleep if the wakeup was bogus.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The predicate form actually generates a small loop internally; it's slightly *more* code under the hood. It's safer, not cheaper.">
      The predicate form is faster than the plain form on modern CPUs.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="They share one CV. Adding the predicate is what lets one CV handle both — the predicate ORs the conditions.">
      You need a separate CV for shutdown vs work; the predicate ties them together.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A junior dev "simplifies" <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> by replacing both mutexes with one <code class="inline">stateMutex_</code>. The code still compiles and passes its tests. What real-world behaviour is now worse?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Memory layout doesn't change meaningfully; both mutexes were already tiny class members.">
      Memory use roughly doubles &mdash; one big mutex is larger than two small ones.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Deadlock requires holding two locks in opposite orders. With one mutex you can't deadlock that way. The harm is different.">
      The class is now susceptible to deadlock.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. submit() (touching requests_) and drainReplies() (touching replies_) used to be independent. Now they serialise on one mutex even though they touch different data. Under load you get false contention — a measurable but invisible-to-tests slowdown.">
      <code class="inline">submit()</code> and <code class="inline">drainReplies()</code> now contend on the same lock even though they touch different data.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Race conditions come from missing synchronisation, not from having only one lock. With one big lock you tend to have *fewer* races, not more.">
      Race conditions become more likely because one lock has to cover more code.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A new feature lets multiple NPCs talk in parallel by spawning multiple worker threads, all calling <code class="inline">processOne</code>. <code class="inline">submit()</code> still does <code class="inline">notify_one</code>. Is anything wrong?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Nothing's broken. notify_one wakes any one sleeping worker. With several workers all waiting on the same CV, the OS picks one — which is exactly what we want for distributing work fairly.">
      Nothing's wrong. <code class="inline">notify_one</code> wakes any single waiter, which is what we want when handing work to a worker pool.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="notify_all would wake all workers; they'd race for the lock, all but one would find an empty queue, and they'd all go back to sleep. Thundering herd. Worse than notify_one here.">
      You must switch to <code class="inline">notify_all</code> or only the first worker will ever do work.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="You can absolutely share a CV between waiters; that's the point. One CV + many workers is the standard pattern for a worker pool.">
      You need one CV per worker; sharing a CV among workers is undefined behaviour.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Multiple threads holding the same shared_ptr is fine; shared_ptr's control block uses atomics for the refcount. That's not the issue here.">
      Each worker needs its own <code class="inline">std::shared_ptr&lt;ChatRequest&gt;</code> to avoid use-after-free.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

